        "content": "| Category Name | Abbreviation | Investment Limit / Ticket Size | Allotment Methodology (Oversubscribed) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Retail Individual Investor** | **RII** | **Min ₹14k up to Max ₹2,000,000 (₹2 Lakhs)** | **Computerized Random Lottery System** (1 Lot per winner) |\n| **Small Non-Institutional Investor**| **sHNI** | **₹2,00,001 to ₹10,00,000** | Computerized Lottery System (Minimum sHNI Lot) |\n| **Big Non-Institutional Investor** | **bHNI** | **Above ₹10,00,000** | Pro-Rata / Proportionate Basis Allotment |\n| **Qualified Institutional Buyers**| **QIB** | **Institutional Heavy Capital** | Pro-Rata Allocation by Merchant Bankers |\n\n*Crucial Threshold Warning:* Agar aapne Retail Category mein ₹2,00,000 se upar ek rupaye ki bhi bid laga di (e.g. ₹2,00,100), toh aapka application automatically RII category se hat kar **sHNI category** mein chala jaayega. sHNI category mein minimum capital block ₹2 Lakhs hota hai aur allotment mechanics completely change ho jaate hain!\n\n---\n\n### 23.2.4 IPO Allotment Math: Cut-off Price & SEBI Lottery System\n\nYe samjhna stock market primary market ka sabse critical mathematical concept hai. Bohot se beginners sochte hain ki \"Agar main ek hi Demat account se 10 lots apply kar doon, toh mujhe shares milne ki probability 10 guna badh jaayegi.\" **THIS IS A COMPLETE MATHEMATICAL MYTH IN RETAIL CATEGORY!**\n\nAao SEBI ke official allotment algorithm aur probability mathematics ko step-by-step decode karte hain.\n\n#### Golden Rule 1: Always Bid at \"Cut-off Price\"!\nIPO price band mein ek Floor Price hoti hai aur ek Cap Price hoti hai (e.g. ₹100 se ₹108). Cap Price (₹108) ko Cut-off Price kehte hain. Always select **\"Cut-off Price\"** option in your broker app. Agar aapne custom lower price (e.g. ₹104) par bid submit ki aur final IPO discovery price ₹108 fix hui, toh aapka application lottery pool mein enter hone se pehle hi instant reject ho jaayega!\n\n#### Golden Rule 2: SEBI Retail Allotment Mechanics in Oversubscribed IPOs\n\nSuppose ek Mainboard IPO aaya jisme Retail Portion mein:\n- Total Retail Lots Available = **1,00,000 Lots**\n- Total Retail Applications Received = **20,000,000 Lots** (IPO is **20x Oversubscribed**)\n\nSEBI Rule ke mutabiq, jab retail category oversubscribe hoti hai, toh Priority Rule active hota hai: **\"Maximum unique retail applicants ko at least 1 Minimum Lot milna chahiye.\"**\n\nComputerized Random Lottery Draw run kiya jata hai:\n- System Total Valid Unique PAN Applications ki list banata hai.\n- System randomly 1,00,000 lucky unique PAN entries choose karta hai.\n- Every lucky winner gets **EXACTLY 1 LOT (Regardless of whether they applied for 1 Lot or 13 Lots!).**\n\n#### Mathematical Proof: Single Account Multi-Lot Fallacy vs Multi-Account Family Strategy\n\nAao 19-saal ke student **Rahul** ki mathematical probability calculate karte hain:\n\n##### Scenario A: Single Demat Account — Multi-Lot Bidding\n- Rahul applies **5 Lots** from HIS SINGLE DEMAT ACCOUNT (Capital Blocked: $5 \\times ₹15,000 = ₹75,000$).\n- SEBI Rule: Single PAN se submitted saare lots ko merge karke lottery pool mein **1 Single Application Entry** mana jata hai.\n- Let Total Retail Lots Available ($L$) = 50,000 Lots.\n- Total Unique Retail PAN Applications Received ($N$) = 1,000,000.\n\n$$\\text{Rahul's Probability of Allotment } (P_{\\text{single}}) = \\frac{L}{N} = \\frac{50,000}{1,000,000} = 0.05 = \\mathbf{5\\%}$$\n\n*Conclusion:* Rahul ne ₹75,000 block kiye, lekin uski allotment probability 5% hi rahi — jo us bande ke barabar hai jisne sirf 1 lot (₹15,000) apply kiya tha! **₹60,000 Capital completely wasted!**\n\n##### Scenario B: Multi-Account Family Strategy\n- Rahul same ₹60,000 - ₹75,000 capital ko use karke **4 Different Family Member Accounts** (Self, Mother, Father, Sister) se 1-1 Lot bid karta hai.\n- Total Unique PAN Applications in Lottery Pool = **4 Independent Entries**.\n- Single Application Failure Probability ($P_{\\text{fail}}$) = $1 - 0.05 = 0.95$ (95%).\n- Probability of ALL 4 Accounts Failing Simultaneously:\n\n$$P_{\\text{all fail}} = (P_{\\text{fail}})^4 = (0.95)^4 = 0.8145 \\text{ (81.45\\%)}$$\n\n- Probability of Getting **AT LEAST ONE ALLOTMENT** ($P_{\\text{at least 1}}$):\n\n$$P_{\\text{at least 1}} = 1 - P_{\\text{all fail}} = 1 - 0.8145 = 0.1855 = \\mathbf{18.55\\%!}$$\n\n```\n                                ALLOTMENT PROBABILITY COMPARISON\n                                  (In 20x Oversubscribed IPO)\n   20% ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n       │                                                                  │ 18.55%\n   15% │                                                                  │ ┌───────┐\n       │                                                                  │ │       │\n   10% │                                                                  │ │       │\n   10% │                                                                  │ │       │\n    5% │ 5.00%                   5.00%                   9.75%            │ │       │\n       │ ┌───────┐               ┌───────┐               ┌───────┐        │ │       │\n    0% └─┴───────┴───────────────┴───────┴───────────────┴───────┴────────┴─┴───────┴─\n         1 Account (1 Lot)       1 Account (5 Lots)      2 Accounts (1 Lot) 4 Accounts (1 Lot)\n         [Capital: ₹15k]         [Capital: ₹75k]         [Capital: ₹30k]    [Capital: ₹60k]\n         (Baseline Odds)         (WASTED CAPITAL!)       (2x Probability)   (3.7x PROBABILITY!)\n```\n\n#### Expected Value (EV) Math Formula for IPO Gains:\n\n$$\\text{Expected Value (EV)} = P_{\\text{allotment}} \\times \\text{Expected Listing Gain Amount}$$\n\n*Example:* If Expected Listing Gain is ₹9,000 (+60%) and single account probability is 5%, EV = $0.05 \\times 9000 = \\mathbf{₹450}$.\nWith 4 Family Accounts (18.55% probability), EV = $0.1855 \\times 9000 = \\mathbf{₹1,669.50}$! Using family accounts increases your mathematically expected return per IPO by 3.7 times!\n\n#### Multi-Account Family Allotment Probability Matrix\n\n| Strategy Executed | Total Capital Blocked | Total Unique PANs Used | Effective Odds of At Least 1 Allotment (20x Oversubscribed) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Single Account - 1 Lot** | ₹15,000 | 1 PAN | **5.00%** (Baseline Odds) |\n| **Single Account - 5 Lots** | ₹75,000 | 1 PAN | **5.00% (Capital Block Wasted!)** |\n| **Family Strategy - 2 Accounts**| ₹30,000 | 2 PANs (Self + Dad) | **9.75%** |\n| **Family Strategy - 4 Accounts**| ₹60,000 | 4 PANs (Self, Dad, Mom, Sister)| **18.55% (3.7x Better Odds!)** |\n| **Family Strategy - 6 Accounts**| ₹90,000 | 6 PANs (Extended Family) | **26.49% (5.3x Better Odds!)** |\n\n---\n\n### 23.2.5 Listing Gains Strategy vs Long-Term Holding Strategy\n\nListing Day par (usually IPO close hone ke 3 working days baad) morning 10:00 AM par stock exchange (NSE/BSE) par shares live trading ke liye list ho jaate hain.",
